$FG,5$$TX+CX,"Influences — On the Voice of ZionOS"$$FG$

$FG,2$On the dub tradition$FG$

ZionOS owes its lyrical posture to the broader roots-reggae and dub
tradition that grew out of Jamaica in the 1960s and 1970s — the
Nyabinghi drummers and chanters of the Rastafari movement, the
producers and engineers who treated the mixing board as an
instrument, and the dub-poets and toasters who shaped the cadence of
Caribbean English into a liturgy of resistance.

The DubPsalms in ::/Adam/Jah/DubPsalms.DD are original compositions.
They do not quote or paraphrase any artist's lyrics. They sit in the
tradition, not on top of any one record.

------------------------------------------------------------

$FG,2$On Lee "Scratch" Perry (1936-2021)$FG$

Producer, engineer, and dub innovator. Founder of the Black Ark
studio in Washington Gardens, Kingston. His treatment of the
recording studio as a sacred and elemental space — and his framing
of music, technology, and trouble in spiritual terms — is part of
why a project that puts an oracle inside an operating system has any
plausible voice at all.

The following short remarks are quoted with attribution from a
verified primary source. They are not lyrics.

$FG,4$From a 2009 interview with Mike Diver, Clash Magazine:$FG$

  $FG,3$"Music is a gift from God and you must use it wisely."$FG$

  $FG,3$"Technology is a gift from God, and we should give thanks
   for technology."$FG$

  $FG,3$"I am the upsetter; but I don't want to hurt anyone."$FG$

  Source: Mike Diver, "Lee 'Scratch' Perry Interview,"
  Clash Magazine, 27 April 2009.

------------------------------------------------------------

$FG,2$On framing$FG$

Perry is named here as an influence and quoted in his own words.
None of the Psalms in this directory are derived from his lyrics or
those of any other artist. ZionOS holds him in the same place this
operating system has long held its other antecedents: gratefully,
and without imitation.

Jah Rastafari.
